/// Vector constants (zero, dirX, dirY, dirZ) and matrix-times-vector operations
/// that return vectors, such as `vector.multiplyPoint`.


// Vector Library (vector)
//
// The vector-valued constants (zero, dirX, dirY, dirZ), and operations that
// take a matrix and/or vectors and return a VECTOR. The operation namespace
// exists to disambiguate overloads by return type: matrix×matrix is
// `matrix.multiply` (returns a matrix), while matrix×vector is
// `vector.multiply` (returns a vector). GSL cannot overload on argument type,
// so the package name carries the distinction.
//
// A matrix here is the same column-vector list the matrix library uses: a
// list of C column vectors, each a vecR, is an R×C matrix.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// =========================================================
// CONSTANTS
// =========================================================

// The origin and the standard basis. builtin's axis-aligned primitives
// (axisX, cylinderZ, ...) are defined in terms of these.

/// The origin, [0, 0, 0]. Also the zero vector for direction arguments.
zero = [0, 0, 0];
/// Unit vector along +X.
dirX = [1, 0, 0];
/// Unit vector along +Y.
dirY = [0, 1, 0];
/// Unit vector along +Z.
dirZ = [0, 0, 1];

// =========================================================
// PRODUCTS
// =========================================================

/// multiply(m, v) is the matrix–vector product m·v. For an R×C matrix m and a
/// vecC v it returns the vecR formed as the linear combination of m's columns
/// weighted by the components of v. (Same lowering as matrix.multiply, one
/// column of b.)
multiply(m, v) {
  scaled = map((col, k) { return col * v[k]; }, m);
  return reduce((acc, c) { return acc + c; }, scaled);
}

/// multiplyPoint(m, point) applies the 4×4 homogeneous transform m to a vec3
/// POINT — i.e. with w = 1, so translation is included — and returns the vec3
/// result. To map a world point into a shape's local frame, pass
/// matrix.inverse(transform): `remap(shape(), vector.multiplyPoint(inv, p))`.
multiplyPoint(m, point) {
  h = multiply(m, [point[0], point[1], point[2], 1]);
  return [h[0], h[1], h[2]];
}

/// multiplyVector(m, dir) applies the 4×4 homogeneous transform m to a vec3
/// DIRECTION — i.e. with w = 0, so translation is ignored — and returns the
/// vec3 result. This is correct for tangents/ray directions; transforming a
/// surface NORMAL under a non-rigid transform instead needs the
/// inverse-transpose of the linear block.
multiplyVector(m, dir) {
  h = multiply(m, [dir[0], dir[1], dir[2], 0]);
  return [h[0], h[1], h[2]];
}

// =========================================================
// ARITHMETIC OPERATIONS
// =========================================================

/// addVector(a, b) computes the componentwise sum of two vectors.
addVector(a, b) {
  return map((x, i) { return x + b[i]; }, a);
}

/// addScalar(v, s) adds a scalar to each component of vector v.
addScalar(v, s) {
  return map((x) { return x + s; }, v);
}

/// subtractVector(a, b) computes the componentwise difference a - b.
subtractVector(a, b) {
  return map((x, i) { return x - b[i]; }, a);
}

/// subtractScalarFrom(v, s) subtracts scalar s from each component of vector v.
subtractScalarFrom(v, s) {
  return map((x) { return x - s; }, v);
}

/// subtractFromScalar(s, v) computes the componentwise difference s - v.
subtractFromScalar(s, v) {
  return map((x) { return s - x; }, v);
}

/// multiplyScalar(v, s) multiplies each component of vector v by scalar s.
multiplyScalar(v, s) {
  return map((x) { return x * s; }, v);
}

/// multiplyComponents(a, b) computes the elementwise product of two vectors.
multiplyComponents(a, b) {
  return map((x, i) { return x * b[i]; }, a);
}

/// divideByScalar(v, s) divides each component of vector v by scalar s.
divideByScalar(v, s) {
  return map((x) { return x / s; }, v);
}

/// divideScalarBy(s, v) divides scalar s by each component of vector v.
divideScalarBy(s, v) {
  return map((x) { return s / x; }, v);
}

/// divideComponents(a, b) divides vector a by vector b elementwise.
divideComponents(a, b) {
  return map((x, i) { return x / b[i]; }, a);
}

/// negate(v) negates each component of vector v.
negate(v) {
  return map((x) { return -x; }, v);
}

